      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I agree with OP, and have some additional feedback i'd like to add. i think i have a general idea on how physbone works, and what makes it more performant than dynamicbone.\n\nIn most use cases, I understand that the animating of physbone properties across the board would cause a detrimental performance hit. So, how can we work around this to keep them performant but still have some animation-compatible properties?\n\nProposed solutions for devs to consider:\n\n  1: A tickbox for individual property values: users would need to set a property as animatable in the SDK for it to respond to animations in-world. I don't know if the performance hit is an all or nothing thing, but this may be a way to go. i feel option 1 or 1a will need be used in tandem with any other solution proposed. \n\n  1a: Alternatively, when uploading (or loading), the SDK (or client) could do a check through the animation layers for any animations that affect physbone components, and automatically set them as needed, up to a defined limit.\n\nBonus: this could also be an elegant solution to the 'is animated' tickbox for the bone itself, if animations referencing the transforms are additionally checked for. This guarantees that only the needed bones are using the option, avoids possible issues with people who would just check every box even if it isn't needed.\n\n  2: Enabling property change animations at a reduced update rate that can be deemed performant could be investigated for viability. while i'd like to see at least 15 per second, many use cases would still be fine with as few as 2 or 3.\n\n  3: Limit how many of the variables can be animated on any given bone to help maintain a performant environment. i prefer to see 4 or more values on a given physbone, but could settle for as few as 2\n\n  4: Limit the number of animation compatible values on a per avatar basis, and affect the avatar perf. rating at intervals.\n\n  5: Bake the relevant animation into the initial physbone behavior calculation? this one pushes the limits of my understanding of physbones, might not make any sense, may cause performance spikes when loading the avatar, or may dramatically increase resource usage of physbones. I thought I'd mention it anyway.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
